A||||15||skitime||Will dealers repair a car that has't had recall work?||||12||
Z||000000||skitime||03-06-2001||10:10 PM||||I just purchased a red 86 GT. I was thinking of it as a parts car but it is cleaning up nicely. I didn't think we need three GT Fieros. There is a cure for this sickness isn't there? Anyway, this car still has the trunk seal and no manifold shield.  Will the dealers still perform the recall work? ||||reg||12
Z||000001||DJRice||03-06-2001||10:48 PM||||Recalls dont expire, the dealer doesnt really have a choice.  If they refuse you can call GM and get them in a boatload of trouble.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||03-07-2001||06:43 AM||||Is there somewhere that says what work should be done with this recall?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||03-07-2001||08:15 AM||||I had some recall work done on my 88GT a year or so back and the dealer said that he got one of the last recall kits left from GM.||||reg||1
Z||000004||firstfiero||03-07-2001||09:14 AM||||Are their any recalls for an 86 se...||||reg||4
Z||000005||rogergarrison||03-07-2001||09:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR><B>Are their any recalls for an 86 se...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>my 86 SE had two done, exhaust manifold replacement and E brake cables. Free from local Pontiac dealer. I dont know why a dealer wouldnt want to do it, manufacturer pays him to do it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||03-07-2001||10:32 AM||||What did they do during the manifold replacement?  Both manifolds? All I am able to find out so far on the net is the recall number is 88C24.  What does the recall say?||||reg||1
Z||000007||skitime||03-07-2001||10:59 AM||||Found this information:<P>NHTSA CAMPAIGN ID Number: 89V232000<BR>Component: ENGINE<BR>Manufacturer: GENERAL MOTORS CORP.<BR>Potential Number of Units Affected: 244000<BR>Make: PONTIAC<BR>Model: FIERO<BR>Manufactured From: JUL 1984 To: AUG 1988 <BR>Year of Recall: '90<BR>Type of Report: Vehicle <BR>Summary:<BR>PCV GROMMET OIL LEAKS, TRANSMISSION OIL COOLER LEAKS, PISTON CONNECTING ROD FAILURES BREAKING THROUGH ENGINE WALL CAUSING OIL SPILLAGE, FUEL RAIL LEAKS, COOLANT LEAKS, ROCKER COVER OIL LEAKS, AND MAINTENANCE OR SERVICE RELATED FACTORS, COULD CAUSE ENGINE COMPARTMENT FIRES. <P>AN ENGINE COMPARTMENT FIRE CAN SPREAD TO THE PASSENGER COMPARTMENT AND INJURE OCCUPANTS. <P>CORRECT OR INSTALL NEW COMPONENTS TO PREVENT UNREASONABLE FIRE RISK. <P>SYSTEM: ENGINE. <P>VEHICLE DESCRIPTION: FIERO PASSENGER CARS WITH 2.8L 6 CYLINDER ENGINES. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||saleenfiero||03-08-2001||01:15 AM||||Okay, I know nothing about how recall repairs work.  Whats the catch here?  How do you know if recall work's been done if you're like the third of 4th owner?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jake_Dragon||03-08-2001||06:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>PISTON CONNECTING ROD FAILURES BREAKING THROUGH ENGINE WALL CAUSING OIL SPILLAGE<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So that’s what is causing all of my oil leaks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||GarethWright||03-08-2001||07:12 PM||||A good thing to check to see if your recalls have been done is the weatherstrip on the front of the trunk/engine lid.  It should be gone, there will be 3 little holes where it used to be attached.  I also think that the tubes on the trunk firewall are recall repairs.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||03-08-2001||07:58 PM||||call your dealer, hell ask for your vin # off dash. They keep a record on computer of recall work done, so theyll know if anyone has done it or not. They replaced forward manifold, replaced all the broken bolts  in head with higher grade studs and flange nuts. also replace entire E brake cable assby. It had already had the rest done prior by previous owner.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Nashco||03-08-2001||08:57 PM||||If you're not sure, don't rely on the dealership to look in the computer!!!!!!!!!!!!<P>I jumped through hoops for about a month after buying my 88 GT that hadn't had the recall work done in 155K miles!  I had to do a lot of research and finally got the dealership to look at it, even though the computer said it had been done.  They told me they looked at it, and it had been done.<P>I WAS VERY UPSET, BECAUSE THEY HADN'T LOOKED AT IT AT ALL!  After about five minutes of "disagreement" I asked if I could show them what hadn't been done, to clearly show them.  After pointing out EVERY thing that hadn't been done, they finally admitted it hadn't been done.  The reason they didn't want to believe me is 1)Since the computer says it has been done, they have to get special permission to get reimbursed 2)They think because I'm a kid I don't know any better (?) 3)They didn't believe that this work had never been done; after all, the computer says it has.<P>The computers are out of date, they don't record the actual information for the car anymore.  None of the info whatsoever was in the computer anymore: seller, date sold, location built, work performed by dealer, etc.<P>I didn't save the pages I used to access the info, but you can search google.com for fiero recall info.  You can also get the recall info upon request by calling 1-800-PMCARES and asking it to be faxed to your local dealer.<P>I'm about to find out (next month when I get back to Alabama) how much they're going to repair, and what the availability is for these parts, if you want an update then, let me know. <P>GOOD LUCK; you'll need it,<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>P.S. Remember to be patient and courteous at all times, no matter how troublesome things get.  Nobody's out to get you here <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||11
Z||000013||skitime||03-08-2001||09:47 PM||||Thanks for good information. I am sure it will be a battle to get the work done. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||rockcrawl||03-08-2001||10:17 PM||||I don't think it's worth fighting with the dealer for. Considering the age of the car, I can understand why they wouldn't want to do any recall work. The dealer does get paid for warranty and recall work, but not nearly as much as they'd be making on a non-warranty job. I guess it's still worth a trip to the dealer to see what they have to say about it, but I wouldn't go out of my way to get it done. If the Fiero owner (at the time the recall was issued) would not have ignored the letter that GM sent him, the work would have been done promptly. <P>I'm doing a SC3800 swap for a guy here and I was surprized to see the valve cover still connected to the air cleaner on his GT with over 150,000 miles on the car!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||03-09-2001||06:45 AM||||Rock, working with dealers everyday the way I do, I find usually the biggest problems getting the recall work done is with the mechanics not the service reps. Its true they dont get paid as much for warranty work but seems to me theres always a mechanic standing around with his hands up his b--t. They work on flat rate and they dont want to mess with a job they have to bust there azz on that there not getting paid much to do. The more work they can get done faster than book calls for is more in thier pocket. As we know on Fiero 'everything' alway ends up taking longer than expected. The mechanic who replaced my manifold worked on the job for 6 hours and Id be willing to bet they didnt pay him $400 to do it-the book calls for less than 2 hours-so thats all hed get paid to do it. Another point to make is that a lot of the mechanics at dealers now arent familiar with working on Fieros any more either.||||reg||1
